# Commercial S9 timer liveness — 2026-09-03

## Scope

Read-only Night Shift III verification found that the S9 audience, nurture and
health timers were `active` and `enabled`, but each exposed an empty
`NextElapseUSecRealtime` and `NextElapseUSecMonotonic=infinity`. Their most
recent triggers were 2026-09-02 15:24:38 UTC, 2026-08-31 18:19:38 UTC and
2026-09-02 15:33:54 UTC respectively. The daily report timer, which already
uses `OnCalendar`, retained a future trigger.

No token, message, media, Telegram identity or personal data was read. The
public posting path was not invoked.

## Cause and correction

The three affected timers used `OnUnitActiveSec`. That monotonic anchor is no
longer present in the loaded service state, so systemd has no future elapse to
schedule even though the timer units remain active. This is a silent liveness
failure that the prior active/enabled-only checks did not detect.

The SSOT timers now use persistent calendar anchors:

- audience: every 15 minutes at minutes 00, 15, 30 and 45;
- nurture: every 15 minutes at minutes 03, 18, 33 and 48;
- health: every 5 minutes.

The stagger preserves bounded one-shot execution while removing dependence on
the backing service's volatile activation timestamp. `systemd-analyze
calendar` on the target host validated all three expressions and produced
successive future elapses.

## Risk and rollback

Risk is low and limited to the existing public-SFW automation. The change does
not alter service commands, Telegram credentials, content, rate limits,
product gates, adult-media boundaries, AVS, payment or publishing providers.
Installing it requires a controlled daemon reload and timer re-arm, which is
not performed during the unattended window because privileged access is not
available non-interactively.

Rollback is the previous canonical Control commit plus restoration of the
three prior timer files from the existing public-runtime recovery point. No
database rollback is required.

## Initial deployment decision

`WAITING_OPERATOR_S9_TIMER_LIVENESS_DEPLOY`

The fix is safe to version and review. Server installation remains pending one
privileged maintenance window with pre-check, existing recovery verification,
daemon reload, timer re-arm, next-elapse verification and public health check.

The follow-up liveness guard makes both the S9 and active S10 public health
envelopes reject an active/enabled timer that has neither a realtime nor a
monotonic future elapse. A dedicated backup-first repair entrypoint now bundles
the three calendar-unit installations, health-envelope refresh, daemon reload,
timer re-arm, next-elapse proof, public health check and automatic file
rollback. It does not use `restart`, does not post content and does not alter
the database, credentials, Adult gates, AVS, payment or publishing state.

The repair entrypoint still requires one explicit privileged execution after
the final canonical Control revision has been synchronized to the server. No
credential prompt or server mutation is attempted during the unattended
window.

## S10.2A fail-closed installation finding

The first controlled S10.2A installation attempt reached the versioned health
gate and was rolled back automatically. The installed S9 health unit predated
the S8 Telegram credential binding already present in Control SSOT. The repair
entrypoint refreshed the hash-bound health scripts but not their base health
unit, so the service could not provide `s8_telegram_token` to the existing S10
drop-in. The Control checkout returned to its verified pre-attempt commit and
the captured systemd files were restored byte-for-byte.

The bounded correction adds that single S9 health unit to the same source
validation, backup, installation and rollback surfaces as the three timers and
health scripts. It introduces no new runtime, credential, provider or product
capability.

The first natural S10.2A audience trigger then exposed a second stale installed
unit: the server's audience service predated its already-versioned S8 Telegram
credential binding, while the nurture service matched SSOT and completed its
natural run successfully. The recovery surface now includes the audience unit
as well. The natural health trigger also proved that a timer can temporarily
report no next elapse while its own service is running. The S9 health timer is
therefore offset from audience and nurture, and its self-trigger is identified
without weakening the independent S10 health guard, which continues to reject
any S9 timer without a future elapse.

The first installation attempt of that natural-run correction then failed
closed before acceptance because the S10 health launcher still hash-bound the
previous S9 health implementation. The launcher binding is refreshed to the
reviewed S9 health SHA-256, and a regression test now requires both artifacts
to remain cryptographically aligned.

The subsequent bounded acceptance exposed the equivalent transient in the S10
health timer itself: while its service was running, the `OnUnitActiveSec`
schedule correctly reported no next elapse until service completion, causing
the health envelope to reject its own healthy in-flight invocation. The S10
health envelope now applies the same narrow self-trigger exception: only its
own timer, only while systemd reports `SubState=running`. A waiting timer with
no future elapse remains a hard failure. This does not relax checks for any S9
timer or for the S10 timer outside its own active health invocation.

## Review tooling note

The first read-only PR inspection omitted the required PR selector while using
the repository flag. GitHub rejected only that inspection command; PR #93 had
already been created successfully and no repository content changed. The
inspection was repeated with the explicit PR number.

## Final S10.2A decision

`AUTOMATED_WMS_SFW_GROWTH = GO`

The backup-first repair and final self-timer correction were installed from
canonical Control. Audience, nurture, S9 health and S10 health each completed a
natural successful run, and every recurring timer exposed a future elapse.
Public WMS health remained green, baseline services retained `NRestarts=0`,
Adult runtimes remained inactive and both server repositories were clean.
The aggregated, token-free operational record is versioned in
`docs/COMMERCIAL_S10_2A_S9_TIMER_RECOVERY_2026-09-03.evidence`.
